{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-225",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Trace the source forward-bias circuit, including series ammeter, parallel voltmeter, adjustable-resistor wiper, all branches and signed open supply terminals.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/9p/figures/fig-225.png",
      "sha256": "afe52fd1713f22579bf2928db1dc3c9b24f61d3b974658e1415d0f4b43544951"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "111. Current across the junction between p-type and n-type semiconductors"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "In Figure 224, the left half of the sample contains donor impurities and is an $n$-type semiconductor.\nFree electrons are the more numerous charge carriers there.\n\nThe right half contains acceptor impurities and is a $p$-type semiconductor, where holes are the more numerous charge carriers.\nA hole is a vacancy left by an electron, and its movement can also produce current.\n\nWhen the two semiconductor types are adjacent, their boundary is called a $p-n$ junction.\nThese letters identify the conduction types.\n\nThey do not mean that the two whole regions carry negative and positive net charges, respectively.\nAfter the regions are joined, electrons and holes diffuse from the side where each is more numerous toward the side where it is less numerous.\n\nNear the boundary, electrons fill holes, leaving charged impurity ions that cannot move freely.\nThese ions produce a built-in electric field.\n\nThe field opposes further diffusion of majority carriers and creates a built-in potential barrier, an energy threshold for crossing the boundary.\nWithout an external power supply, once equilibrium is reached, diffusion and the opposing motion caused by the field balance.\nThere is no sustained net current."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "Figure 224"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "Figure 225"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "Connect the sample as in Figure 225: the battery's positive terminal to the $p$-type side and its negative terminal to the $n$-type side.\nThe first side is at a positive potential and the second at a negative potential, so the first side has the higher potential.\n\nThe applied voltage reduces the barrier that previously opposed diffusion.\nThe region near the boundary with few free charge carriers also becomes narrower.\n\nCurrent through the $p-n$ junction is now mainly produced by majority carriers crossing the boundary.\nElectrons diffuse from the $n$ region to the $p$ region, and holes diffuse from the $p$ region to the $n$ region.\nThe battery continually supplies charge, allowing the process to continue rather than occurring only at the instant of connection.\n\nElectrons move opposite to the conventional current direction.\nHoles move in the same direction as conventional current.\nWith this connection, the sample conducts readily and has a relatively small resistance."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "This connection is called forward bias, and the current is called forward current.\nFigure 226a shows the forward current-voltage characteristic: the relationship between current and applied voltage."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "This lesson considers only reverse voltages too small to cause breakdown.\nA sufficiently large reverse voltage can produce a large reverse current.\nThis is called reverse breakdown and is outside this lesson's scope.\n\nReverse the battery connections: connect the side previously attached to the positive terminal to the negative terminal, and vice versa.\nFor the same voltage magnitude, the circuit current is much smaller than with forward bias.\n\nThe applied voltage now increases the built-in barrier, and the region near the boundary with few free carriers becomes wider.\nIt is harder for majority carriers to cross the boundary.\n\nA small current can still flow, mainly because of the other type of carrier, which is less numerous.\nElectrons cross from the $p$ region to the $n$ region, and holes cross from the $n$ region to the $p$ region.\nFree electrons are less numerous in a $p$-type semiconductor.\n\nHoles are less numerous in an $n$-type semiconductor.\nThese less numerous carriers are called minority carriers.\n\nThe number of majority carriers therefore cannot be used to estimate the current crossing the boundary here.\nWithin the operating range considered in this lesson, the sample conducts poorly and has a very large reverse resistance.\nThe current is small, but it is not entirely absent.\n\nThis small current is called reverse current;\nits current-voltage characteristic appears in Figure 226b.\n\nFigure 226c combines the forward and reverse current-voltage characteristics of the $p-n$ junction.\nFigures 226a, b, and c explain the processes at the boundary between $p$-type and $n$-type semiconductors only qualitatively.\nThey show trends, not exact current or voltage values that can be read from the diagrams.\n\nThe actual forward and reverse currents differ greatly.\nDo not treat these schematic diagrams as measurements showing currents of similar magnitude."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "Figure 226"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n111",
        "text": "**A $p-n$ junction behaves differently for the two directions of current.\nThe forward resistance of a $p-n$ junction is much smaller than its reverse resistance.**\n\nThis asymmetry of the $p-n$ junction can be used to rectify alternating current.\nAlternating current repeatedly changes direction.\n\nThe junction readily allows forward current but greatly reduces reverse current, producing current that mainly flows in one direction.\nThe rectified current may still vary with time.\nA mostly consistent direction does not by itself mean a constant current magnitude."
      }
    ],
